{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "patient-care",
    "title": "Patient Care",
    "order": 4
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "pharmacology-contrast-media",
    "title": "Pharmacology & Contrast Media",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "Why This Matters for RTTs",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Radiation therapists aren't pharmacists, but contrast media and basic pharmacology come up regularly \u2014 contrast-enhanced CT simulation scans, patients on medications that affect treatment scheduling, and recognizing when a patient is having an adverse reaction. You need working knowledge, not prescribing authority."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Types of Contrast Media",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Contrast media are classified by how they interact with X-rays:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Negative contrast agents",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 air or gas, which appear dark on imaging (used less often in RT simulation)"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Positive contrast agents",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 appear bright/white on imaging: "
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Barium sulfate",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 used for GI tract studies, not injected"
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "hardBreak"
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Water-soluble iodinated contrast",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 injected intravenously for CT simulation to help distinguish vessels, organs, and some tumors from surrounding tissue"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Ionic vs. Non-Ionic Iodinated Contrast",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Water-soluble iodinated contrast comes in two chemical classes:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Ionic contrast",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " dissociates into charged particles in solution \u2014 higher osmolality, generally higher reaction risk, and increasingly less common in modern practice."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Non-ionic contrast",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " does not dissociate \u2014 lower osmolality, better tolerated, and the standard choice in most facilities today."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Appropriateness of Contrast to the Procedure",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Before contrast is given, several patient factors need to be checked:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Patient condition",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 known allergies, prior reactions to contrast"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Age and weight",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 dosing considerations"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Laboratory values",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 particularly "
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "BUN, creatinine, and eGFR",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": ", since iodinated contrast is cleared by the kidneys and can cause contrast-induced nephropathy in patients with impaired renal function"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page4_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Which lab values are most relevant before administering iodinated contrast?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Hemoglobin and hematocrit",
              "BUN, creatinine, and eGFR",
              "Platelet count"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "BUN, creatinine, and eGFR reflect kidney function, which determines how safely a patient can clear iodinated contrast."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Contrast Reactions",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Local effects",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " \u2014 extravasation/infiltration (contrast leaking into surrounding tissue instead of the vein) and phlebitis (vein inflammation)."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Systemic effects",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ", graded by severity:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Mild",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 nausea, mild hives, warmth/flushing"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Moderate",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 more pronounced hives, wheezing, vomiting"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Severe",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 anaphylaxis, cardiac or respiratory compromise \u2014 a true emergency"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Know your department's emergency medication cart location and your role if a reaction occurs \u2014 typically staying with the patient, calling for help, and documenting exactly what happened and when."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Radiopharmaceuticals",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Radiopharmaceuticals combine a radioactive isotope with a pharmaceutical carrier, used mainly in diagnostic imaging like PET (Positron Emission Tomography) \u2014 FDG-PET is commonly fused with CT simulation datasets for treatment planning."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Because these involve unsealed radioactive sources, special precautions apply: proper handling by qualified personnel, patient-specific radiation safety instructions, and awareness of decay time before certain procedures."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Your Role and Documentation",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "If any adverse reaction occurs \u2014 to contrast, a medication, or anything else \u2014 your responsibilities are the same regardless of severity: recognize it early, get appropriate help immediately, and document exactly what was observed, when, and what response was taken."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page7_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A patient develops hives and mild wheezing shortly after contrast injection. What's your first action?"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "Wait to see if it resolves on its own before doing anything",
              "Stay with the patient and get help/notify the appropriate provider immediately"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Any contrast reaction, even one that looks moderate, needs an immediate response \u2014 stay with the patient and get help right away."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Wrap-Up",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You've covered contrast media types, ionic vs. non-ionic iodinated contrast, appropriateness screening (including renal function labs), reaction severity levels, radiopharmaceuticals, and your role if something goes wrong."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You're not expected to manage a reaction independently \u2014 you're expected to recognize one quickly and get the right help fast."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
